UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 18, Lesson 1: Including others', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Including others means making sure everyone has a chance to take part, feel welcome and belong. We can invite people to join, change games so everyone can play, say names correctly and respect cultural differences and different abilities. Leaving someone out on purpose can make them feel lonely. If you see someone left out, you can invite them, ask what they would like, or tell a trusted adult.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA game needs fast running and one player cannot run quickly. Write two ways to change the game so all players can take part.\n\nFEEDBACK\nIdeas: use walking or hopping, give different jobs such as scorer or goalkeeper, or let teams choose how to move. Changing the game to include everyone is better than leaving someone out.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlan an inclusion week at school: write five actions you could do to welcome others (inviting, learning names, adapting games). For each, say who benefits and how you would respond if someone says ''no thanks''.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Including others means making sure everyone has a chance to take part, feel welcome and belong. We can invite people to join, change games so everyone can play, say names correctly and respect cultural differences and different abilities. Leaving someone out on purpose can make them feel lonely. If you see someone left out, you can invite them, ask what they would like, or tell a trusted adult.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-RRS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Plan an inclusion week at school: write five actions you could do to welcome others (inviting, learning names, adapting games). For each, say who benefits and how you would respond if someone says ''no thanks''. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Ideas: use walking or hopping, give different jobs such as scorer or goalkeeper, or let teams choose how to move. Changing the game to include everyone is better than leaving someone out. Reasoning: I used including others by attending to belonging rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''. Correction: Ideas: use walking or hopping, give different jobs such as scorer or goalkeeper, or let teams choose how to move. Changing the game to include everyone is… Why: the correction restores accurate use of including others.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating including others on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with including others, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Ideas: use walking or hopping, give different jobs such as scorer or goalkeeper, or let teams choose how to move. Changing the game to include everyone is… This matches the lesson skill for including others.","options":["Copy the example and call that independent work.","Ideas: use walking or hopping, give different jobs such as scorer or goalkeeper, or let teams choose how to move. Changing the game to include everyone is…","Thinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"A game needs fast running and one player cannot run quickly. Write two ways to change the game so all players can take part. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Thinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Thinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''.","Including others means making sure everyone has a chance to take part, feel welcome and belong. We can invite people to join, change games so everyone can…","Ideas: use walking or hopping, give different jobs such as scorer or goalkeeper, or let teams choose how to move. Changing the game to include everyone is…","Careful use of belonging with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising including others?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named belonging, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the belonging used."],"question":"A learner is practising including others. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Ideas: use walking or hopping, give different jobs such as scorer or goalkeeper, or let teams choose how to move. Changing the game to include everyone is… uses safe, healthy, respectful choices appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Ideas: use walking or hopping, give different jobs such as scorer or goalkeeper, or let teams choose how to move. Changing the game to include everyone is…","Thinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about including others?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about including others.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about belonging?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example: at lunch Ben sees a new student alone. He walks over, says ''Hi, I''m Ben. Would you like to play with us?'' and tells the group the…","Thinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Including others means making sure everyone has a chance to take part, feel welcome and belong. We can… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Thinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''.","Plan an inclusion week at school: write five actions you could do to welcome others (inviting, learning names, adapting games). For each, say who…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example and call that independent work."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of including others?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating including others on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Ideas: use walking or hopping, give different jobs such as scorer or goalkeeper, or let teams choose how to move. Changing the game to include everyone is better than leaving someone out. Reasoning: I used including others by attending to belonging rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with including others, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''. Correction: Ideas: use walking or hopping, give different jobs such as scorer or goalkeeper, or let teams choose how to move. Changing the game to include everyone is… Why: the correction restores accurate use of including others.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w18-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise including others.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Health, Wellbeing and Relationships","success_criteria":["I can use including others with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify belonging and explain why it matters.","I can practise adapting a game independently on a new prompt.","I can notice inviting kindly needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Including others means making sure everyone has a chance to take part, feel welcome and belong. We can invite people to join, change games so everyone can play, say names correctly and respect cultural differences and different abilities. Leaving someone out on purpose can make…","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Including others means making sure everyone has a chance to take part, feel welcome and belong. We can invite people to join, change games so everyone can play, say names correctly and respect cultural differences and different abilities. Leaving someone out on purpose can make them feel lonely. If you see someone left out, you can invite them, ask what they would like, or tell a trusted adult.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about including others?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: at lunch Ben sees a new student alone. He walks over, says ''Hi, I''m Ben. Would you like to play with us?'' and tells the group the new student''s name. When the student says they would rather watch today, Ben says ''That''s fine, you can join any time.''\n\nTRY FIRST\nA game needs fast running and one player cannot run quickly. Write two ways to change the game so all players can take part.\n\nFEEDBACK\nIdeas: use walking or hopping, give different jobs such as scorer or goalkeeper, or let teams choose how to move. Changing the game to include everyone is better than leaving someone out.","explain":"Including others means making sure everyone has a chance to take part, feel welcome and belong. We can invite people to join, change games so everyone can play, say names correctly and respect cultural differences and different abilities. Leaving someone out on purpose can make them feel lonely. If you see someone left out, you can invite them, ask what they would like, or tell a trusted adult.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Inviting and respecting choice.","caption":"Invite, never force.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Invite</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Hi, I am Ben</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Choice</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">you can join any time</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: at lunch Ben sees a new student alone. He walks over, says ''Hi, I''m Ben. Would you like to play with us?'' and t Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: at lunch Ben sees a new student alone. He walks over, says ''Hi, I''m Ben. Would you like to play with us?'' and t Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about including others?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: at lunch Ben sees a new student alone. He walks over, says ''Hi, I''m Ben. Would you like to play with us?'' and tells the group the new student''s name. When the student says they would rather watch today, Ben says ''That''s fine, you can join any time.'' Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Left out and included.","caption":"Change the game.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Left out</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">nobody asks</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Included</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">roles for everyone</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about including others?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''. Here is a more helpful example: Example: at lunch Ben sees a new student alone. He walks over, says ''Hi, I''m Ben. Would you like to play with us?'' and tells the group the new student''s name. When the student says they would rather watch today, Ben says ''That''s fine, you can join any time.''\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nIdeas: use walking or hopping, give different jobs such as scorer or goalkeeper, or let teams choose how to move. Changing the game to include everyone is better than leaving someone out.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking that including someone means forcing them to join. Invite kindly, and respect their choice if they say ''not now''. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about including others?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nIncluding others means making sure everyone has a chance to take part, feel welcome and belong. We can invite people to join, change games so everyone can play, say names correctly and respect cultural differences and different abilities. Leaving someone out on purpose can make them feel lonely. If you see someone left out, you can invite them, ask what they would like, or tell a trusted adult. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 18, Lesson 1: Including others","unit_number":4,"unit_title":"Friendships and respect","week":18,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: at lunch Ben sees a new student alone. He walks over, says ''Hi, I''m Ben. Would you like to play with us?'' and tells the group the new student''s name. When the student says they would rather watch today, Ben says ''That''s fine, you can join any time.''\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Inviting and respecting choice.","caption":"Invite, never force.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Invite</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Hi, I am Ben</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Choice</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">you can join any time</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Left out and included.","caption":"Change the game.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Left out</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">nobody asks</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Included</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">roles for everyone</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Yea